1190
00:56:21.800 --> 00:56:24.280
We had a fan question from Kyle T.

1191
00:56:24.540 --> 00:56:26.500
in Houston, Texas, who said,

1192
00:56:26.620 --> 00:56:30.000
did y'all have a safety meeting for Michael's crowd dive?

1193
00:56:30.380 --> 00:56:33.240
By the way, I want you to know, Kyle wrote y'all.

1194
00:56:33.640 --> 00:56:34.460
Of course he did.

1195
00:56:34.540 --> 00:56:35.160
Kyle from Texas.

1196
00:56:35.440 --> 00:56:36.340
That's right, from Houston.

1197
00:56:36.820 --> 00:56:37.500
H-Town.

1198
00:56:37.720 --> 00:56:40.160
Did y'all have a safety meeting for Michael's crowd dive?

1199
00:56:40.260 --> 00:56:41.840
I appreciated that detail, by the way.

1200
00:56:42.420 --> 00:56:44.580
We did have a safety meeting.

1201
00:56:44.580 --> 00:56:47.860
Eric Sulky was our stunt coordinator,

1202
00:56:48.540 --> 00:56:53.960
and Randy Cordray told us that he gave everyone direction on how to catch Steve's dive

1203
00:56:53.960 --> 00:56:57.060
and how to lower him down to the ground safely.

1204
00:56:57.460 --> 00:57:03.840
We also dressed two stunt players as warehouse workers and added them into the crowd.

1205
00:57:04.520 --> 00:57:11.060
Randy said these burly stunt dudes were the main individuals involved in catching and protecting Steve

1206
00:57:11.060 --> 00:57:13.340
and keeping him from crashing to the floor.

1207
00:57:13.520 --> 00:57:14.440
Yeah, it wasn't me.

1208
00:57:14.580 --> 00:57:18.080
It wasn't like me and Kate and you holding Steve up.

1209
00:57:18.240 --> 00:57:18.560
Correct.

1210
00:57:19.360 --> 00:57:22.960
Now, folks, it's time for some karaoke.

1211
00:57:23.680 --> 00:57:24.020
Yeah!

1212
00:57:24.920 --> 00:57:30.400
This is where Dwight is singing Boulevard of Broken Dreams by Green Day.

1213
00:57:30.800 --> 00:57:33.940
Andy joins in in some perfect harmony.

1214
00:57:34.640 --> 00:57:40.500
Boulevard of Broken Dreams is from Green Day's 2004 album, American Idiot.

1215
00:57:40.680 --> 00:57:46.360
So, something that I know, because I'm friends with Billy Joe Armstrong, I cannot say it enough.

1216
00:57:46.420 --> 00:57:47.220
She's blushing again.

1217
00:57:47.220 --> 00:57:49.340
I'm just so tickled every time I can say that.

1218
00:57:49.340 --> 00:57:49.940
She's blushing again.

1219
00:57:49.940 --> 00:58:02.440
Is that Billy had seen The Office, but he recently, in 2019, did a big rewatch of the whole series.

1220
00:58:02.600 --> 00:58:03.300
Oh, nice!

1221
00:58:03.700 --> 00:58:11.160
And I asked him, when you did your rewatch, did you remember that you had given us permission to use this song?

1222
00:58:11.940 --> 00:58:13.320
Here's what he had to say.

1223
00:58:13.320 --> 00:58:30.100
I was binge-watching The Office for the first time, and I was watching that episode, and then all of a sudden, the song came on, and I was completely taken by surprise.

1224
00:58:31.160 --> 00:58:47.320
And, like, I think my face started to get red, and then Andy, he started singing harmonies, which was almost like the—because we do have a musical for American Idiot,

1225
00:58:47.400 --> 00:58:51.540
and he sang the same exact harmony that's in the musical.

1226
00:58:51.780 --> 00:58:54.400
So, it was good.

1227
00:58:54.400 --> 00:58:59.360
Well, I just love it that he was surprised by his own song during that rewatch.

1228
00:58:59.380 --> 00:58:59.740
I know!

1229
00:58:59.880 --> 00:59:00.620
That's so sweet!

1230
00:59:00.880 --> 00:59:03.180
Listen, I think we need to hear this.

1231
00:59:03.320 --> 00:59:06.800
I think we need to hear Dwight and Andy singing Boulevard of Broken DreaMs.

1232
00:59:06.920 --> 00:59:07.600
Oh, for sure.

1233
00:59:08.800 --> 00:59:14.000
Walk a lonely road, only one that I have ever known.

1234
00:59:14.480 --> 00:59:19.840
Don't know where it goes, but it's all to me that I walk alone.

1235
00:59:20.740 --> 00:59:21.480
It's true.

1236
00:59:21.640 --> 00:59:21.940
Yes!

1237
00:59:22.460 --> 00:59:23.680
We all walk alone.

1238
00:59:36.550 --> 00:59:40.300
I love it.

1239
00:59:40.420 --> 00:59:41.280
I love hearing them.

1240
00:59:41.380 --> 00:59:42.980
I could hear them do it, like, every day.

1241
00:59:43.100 --> 00:59:45.120
It would make me happy singing that song.

1242
00:59:45.760 --> 00:59:55.160
Mindy shared in the commentary that she absolutely loved this montage of Christmas kind of all coming together, set to the backdrop of a non-Christmas song.

1243
00:59:55.260 --> 00:59:55.520
Yes.

1244
00:59:55.900 --> 01:00:03.940
And, Jenna, I guess there was a musical interlude to the karaoke, and Ed and Rain had to vamp during it.

1245
01:00:03.960 --> 01:00:05.060
It's so funny.

1246
01:00:05.060 --> 01:00:07.320
It's in the extended producer's cut.

1247
01:00:07.440 --> 01:00:09.120
We have to hear their vamp.

1248
01:00:32.220 --> 01:00:37.200
I mean, I know they already produce the musical version of American Idiot.

1249
01:00:37.200 --> 01:00:42.180
But I'm saying, if you bring it back to the stage, you might want to add this.

1250
01:00:42.600 --> 01:00:43.320
Ed and Rain.

1251
01:00:43.880 --> 01:00:44.920
You might want to.

1252
01:00:44.980 --> 01:00:47.180
I mean, we've all been there.

1253
01:00:47.300 --> 01:00:48.860
We've all picked a song for karaoke.

1254
01:00:48.860 --> 01:00:50.860
And then there's a big musical interlude.

1255
01:00:50.920 --> 01:00:52.060
And you're like, what do I do?

1256
01:00:52.340 --> 01:00:52.660
Yes.

1257
01:00:52.840 --> 01:00:53.960
I'm just kind of bobbing my head.

1258
01:00:53.960 --> 01:00:56.480
Ed, I mean, Ed crushed it.

1259
01:00:56.920 --> 01:01:03.120
Well, there are just a bunch of scenes now of people enjoying Christmas.

1260
01:01:03.740 --> 01:01:06.960
Everyone's getting gifts that are really meaningful to them.

1261
01:01:06.960 --> 01:01:16.060
And finally, Dwight is going to get the last piece of his gift and realizes this whole time he's been making a nutcracker.

1262
01:01:16.680 --> 01:01:17.840
Yes, of course.

1263
01:01:17.840 --> 01:01:20.560
It's like a really cool nutcracker.

1264
01:01:20.860 --> 01:01:23.940
We had a fan question from Lacey L.

1265
01:01:24.140 --> 01:01:25.940
in Grand Prairie, Texas.

1266
01:01:26.360 --> 01:01:29.360
Please, please tell me about Dwight's nutcracker.

1267
01:01:29.540 --> 01:01:30.540
Who came up with it?

1268
01:01:30.660 --> 01:01:31.860
Where did they find it?

1269
01:01:31.960 --> 01:01:33.360
Or did they have to create it?

1270
01:01:33.360 --> 01:01:35.800
I have been looking for it for years.

1271
01:01:36.120 --> 01:01:37.540
Lacey, I thought the same thing.

1272
01:01:37.780 --> 01:01:39.280
I thought, can I get one of these?

1273
01:01:39.820 --> 01:01:44.280
Well, Randy told me that this was not in the first shooting draft of the script.

1274
01:01:44.500 --> 01:01:46.520
This whole runner was added later.

1275
01:01:46.520 --> 01:01:52.560
And he believes that it was the master of the cold opens, Aaron Schur, who wrote this story arc.

1276
01:01:52.760 --> 01:01:57.800
Because Aaron became the point person that they went to to discuss the creation of this prop.

1277
01:01:58.280 --> 01:02:01.160
Randy said when he and Phil first read this story arc,

1278
01:02:01.160 --> 01:02:05.640
they assumed that this contraption was something the writers had seen on the internet somewhere.

1279
01:02:05.980 --> 01:02:08.160
And they wanted to know where do they buy it.

1280
01:02:08.680 --> 01:02:11.220
It turns out, Ange, it did not exist anywhere.

1281
01:02:11.560 --> 01:02:12.780
They made it up?

1282
01:02:12.960 --> 01:02:14.820
Yeah, they made it up.

1283
01:02:14.880 --> 01:02:20.240
I even went to the rewrite of the script, and there is no scripted description of this machine.

1284
01:02:21.000 --> 01:02:28.840
So Phil needed to conceptualize, design, and build a working nutcracker prop

1285
01:02:28.840 --> 01:02:34.300
that looked like it could have been a gun, and he had less than a week to do it.

1286
01:02:34.480 --> 01:02:35.460
Phil Shea.

1287
01:02:35.700 --> 01:02:38.460
This is why he was always running, folks.

1288
01:02:38.640 --> 01:02:38.980
Mm-hmm.

1289
01:02:39.600 --> 01:02:44.300
Phil went to his go-to props manufacturing house, the hand prop room.

1290
01:02:44.300 --> 01:02:50.020
The team at that facility, Randy said, was understandably freaked out at the very short

1291
01:02:50.020 --> 01:02:52.300
time span that they had to create this prop.

1292
01:02:52.740 --> 01:02:57.400
They told Phil to tell Randy to open up the company checkbook.

1293
01:02:57.700 --> 01:03:01.340
They were going to have to put a full team of engineers and builders basically around

1294
01:03:01.340 --> 01:03:03.000
the clock to get this done in time.

1295
01:03:03.420 --> 01:03:06.340
Randy said to their credit, they did a masterful job.

1296
01:03:06.540 --> 01:03:10.020
All in, this prop cost about $19,000.

1297
01:03:11.620 --> 01:03:13.280
Also, it was a one-off.

1298
01:03:14.000 --> 01:03:20.020
He said, to the best of his knowledge, the NBC Universal marketing people never made replicas

1299
01:03:20.020 --> 01:03:21.480
of this for online sales.

1300
01:03:21.740 --> 01:03:22.760
They should have.

1301
01:03:23.100 --> 01:03:24.100
They still can.

1302
01:03:24.260 --> 01:03:27.980
I'm just saying, this would be a hot item at the holidays.

1303
01:03:28.180 --> 01:03:28.400
Yeah.

1304
01:03:28.620 --> 01:03:30.460
Do you know how many people would buy this?

1305
01:03:30.540 --> 01:03:31.140
I would.

1306
01:03:31.280 --> 01:03:32.100
I would, too.

1307
01:03:32.740 --> 01:03:34.320
But do I have to put it together?

1308
01:03:34.900 --> 01:03:35.560
Oh, no.

1309
01:03:35.620 --> 01:03:36.020
Oh, no.

1310
01:03:36.240 --> 01:03:37.080
What if you do?

1311
01:03:37.080 --> 01:03:38.340
That would be the ultimate.

1312
01:03:38.640 --> 01:03:39.140
I know.

1313
01:03:39.140 --> 01:03:44.640
You know, this story reminds me of something that I had a makeup artist tell me once.

1314
01:03:45.180 --> 01:03:48.440
She said, there is a saying in this business.

1315
01:03:49.280 --> 01:03:52.240
It's called fast, cheap, or good.

1316
01:03:52.640 --> 01:03:55.320
And you can only have two of the three things.

1317
01:03:55.780 --> 01:03:59.000
It can be fast and cheap, but it's not going to look good.

1318
01:03:59.380 --> 01:04:02.300
It can be cheap and good, but it's not going to be faSt.

1319
01:04:02.760 --> 01:04:07.060
If you want it to be both fast and good, it's not going to be cheap.

1320
01:04:07.740 --> 01:04:09.460
And that was the case with our nutcracker.

1321
01:04:10.040 --> 01:04:10.940
Fast and good.

1322
01:04:11.320 --> 01:04:12.160
It was not cheap.

1323
01:04:12.180 --> 01:04:13.080
Not going to be cheap.

1324
01:04:13.240 --> 01:04:13.400
Nope.

1325
01:04:13.920 --> 01:04:21.480
Well, Jenna, Mindy also spoke about this scene on the commentary, and she said it was a last-minute rewrite, that it was not in her original draft.

1326
01:04:21.740 --> 01:04:31.560
She said it was written by Aaron Schur and Charlie Grandy, and Mindy said Phil Shea went above and beyond, especially because it was a last-minute prop gag.

1327
01:04:31.560 --> 01:04:39.880
She also commented that it was a note from Greg to add a Dwight-Michael storyline to this episode, and it pays off so nicely at the end.

1328
01:04:40.040 --> 01:04:42.100
It's a moment of redemption for Michael.

1329
01:04:42.760 --> 01:04:43.180
Yes.

1330
01:04:43.620 --> 01:04:46.080
Yeah, he knows Dwight so well.

1331
01:04:46.400 --> 01:04:46.860
Yeah.

1332
01:04:47.140 --> 01:04:47.840
It's a good note.

1333
01:04:48.060 --> 01:04:48.560
Yeah.

1334
01:04:49.340 --> 01:04:51.380
Well, the party is wrapping up.

1335
01:04:51.500 --> 01:04:52.900
Everyone is leaving for the night.

1336
01:04:53.160 --> 01:04:55.360
They're walking out into the parking lot.

1337
01:04:55.460 --> 01:04:56.200
There's snow.

1338
01:04:56.200 --> 01:04:58.520
It feels so Christmassy and festive.

1339
01:04:59.360 --> 01:05:00.240
That's right.

1340
01:05:00.620 --> 01:05:07.800
You know, as you're walking out, Angela, I don't know if you noticed this, but we got a fan question from Kelsey D in Orlando, Florida.

1341
01:05:08.280 --> 01:05:14.220
I noticed when everyone is leaving the office, Angela slips, and Daryl is there to help her.

1342
01:05:14.760 --> 01:05:17.600
Did Angela actually slip, or was that planned?

1343
01:05:17.740 --> 01:05:19.200
Because I love that moment.

1344
01:05:19.360 --> 01:05:22.020
It's so cute and sweet between Angela and Daryl.

1345
01:05:22.560 --> 01:05:23.120
Okay.

1346
01:05:23.520 --> 01:05:26.060
Kelsey, I did actually trip.

1347
01:05:26.640 --> 01:05:28.520
And I grabbed Craig's arm.

1348
01:05:28.660 --> 01:05:32.160
So that quick moment you're seeing, that's us.

1349
01:05:32.400 --> 01:05:32.840
Aw.

1350
01:05:32.980 --> 01:05:34.020
We're not in character.

1351
01:05:34.340 --> 01:05:41.120
And I think Rain must have seen it and improvised the line as Dwight, watch out, it's slippery out here.

1352
01:05:41.480 --> 01:05:44.260
Because that line is not in the shooting draft.

1353
01:05:44.500 --> 01:05:47.680
Me slipping and tripping is obviously not in the shooting draft.

1354
01:05:47.920 --> 01:05:50.580
So, yes, that's just me and Craig.

1355
01:05:50.580 --> 01:05:54.880
And thank goodness he was there, because I guess I turned my ankle or something.

1356
01:05:55.300 --> 01:05:57.420
Well, all of a sudden we hear something.

1357
01:05:57.960 --> 01:05:59.240
It's drummers.

1358
01:05:59.920 --> 01:06:02.620
It's 12 drummers drumming!

1359
01:06:03.040 --> 01:06:06.320
This is Aaron's final Christmas present from Andy.

1360
01:06:06.600 --> 01:06:08.500
Andy is on the cymbals.

1361
01:06:08.780 --> 01:06:10.400
He starts dancing.

1362
01:06:10.460 --> 01:06:12.060
It's so festive.

1363
01:06:12.060 --> 01:06:17.340
We had a fan catch from Rachel I in Phoenix, Arizona, who said,

1364
01:06:17.500 --> 01:06:20.260
There are only 11 people in the drumline.

1365
01:06:20.420 --> 01:06:23.080
Was Andy meant to be the 12th person?

1366
01:06:23.680 --> 01:06:29.460
Yes, Rachel, he was the 12th percussionist in this drumline.

1367
01:06:29.960 --> 01:06:34.600
We also got a question from Maria S in Austin, Texas, who asked,

1368
01:06:34.860 --> 01:06:39.200
What was the process for choosing this group that played the 12 drummers drumming?

1369
01:06:39.200 --> 01:06:40.660
And Emily B.

1370
01:06:40.860 --> 01:06:42.500
from Torrance, California, said,

1371
01:06:42.620 --> 01:06:46.900
I want to give a shout-out to the RCC Marching Tigers,

1372
01:06:46.900 --> 01:06:50.140
who are the drumline featured in this episode.

1373
01:06:50.940 --> 01:06:55.820
RCC has a fall marching band program and also a winter percussion program

1374
01:06:55.820 --> 01:07:00.220
that is one of the best independent performance groups in the country.

1375
01:07:00.400 --> 01:07:04.900
You can see more about them at rccdrums on IG.

1376
01:07:05.360 --> 01:07:06.100
She said,

1377
01:07:06.100 --> 01:07:10.340
My husband is the battery captain head and a ranger for the group,

1378
01:07:10.500 --> 01:07:14.260
which means he writes the music and teaches the students how to play their instruments.

1379
01:07:14.280 --> 01:07:18.680
So it is always so fun when I see RCC on a show.

1380
01:07:18.880 --> 01:07:21.100
They've been on Parks and Rec, too.

1381
01:07:21.700 --> 01:07:24.940
Emily, thank you so much for sending that in.

1382
01:07:25.480 --> 01:07:28.840
Randy confirmed that everything Emily said is correct.

1383
01:07:29.120 --> 01:07:33.260
He said that he would always reach out to a woman named Christine Day.

1384
01:07:33.600 --> 01:07:35.660
She is a music contractor.

1385
01:07:35.660 --> 01:07:40.560
And she finds musical on-screen performers.

1386
01:07:41.060 --> 01:07:43.500
Randy said there was just no one better than her.

1387
01:07:43.960 --> 01:07:45.340
He actually said, quote,

1388
01:07:45.560 --> 01:07:52.120
She is well known across the industry for her cheerful professionalism and ability to grasp the essence of a script

1389
01:07:52.120 --> 01:07:53.900
and the showrunner's intent.

1390
01:07:53.900 --> 01:08:00.680
All in, including Christine's fee, the marching band cost us $9,950.

1391
01:08:01.680 --> 01:08:09.320
Well, I will put the RCC Drums Instagram in our story so you guys can see what they're up to.

1392
01:08:09.540 --> 01:08:11.000
I loved learning all of that.

1393
01:08:11.620 --> 01:08:15.500
Well, we had so much fun on this night with this marching band.

1394
01:08:15.620 --> 01:08:16.640
I'll never forget it.

1395
01:08:16.740 --> 01:08:18.000
I mean, we got to stay late.

1396
01:08:18.000 --> 01:08:19.660
We always love to stay late.

1397
01:08:19.820 --> 01:08:20.820
We did it so rarely.

1398
01:08:20.820 --> 01:08:22.900
And then we had snow.

1399
01:08:23.460 --> 01:08:27.900
We had snow with this, like, amazing drumming as a backdrop.

1400
01:08:28.320 --> 01:08:28.960
Incredible.

1401
01:08:29.340 --> 01:08:32.000
I asked Randy if we used snow business again.

1402
01:08:32.140 --> 01:08:34.440
And he said, no, not this time.

1403
01:08:34.440 --> 01:08:38.320
It was Ron Neri's group, ANA Special Effects in Van Nuys.

1404
01:08:38.920 --> 01:08:46.840
He said, in order to get that snow in our parking lot, they had to call this place Union Ice in North Hollywood,

1405
01:08:46.840 --> 01:08:52.020
and they brought two semi-tractor trailer rigs full of ice.

1406
01:08:52.480 --> 01:08:53.080
Two!

1407
01:08:53.380 --> 01:08:53.900
Wow.

1408
01:08:54.240 --> 01:09:00.580
And then Ron's team put the ice through an industrial chipper, and it, like, shot it into the air.

1409
01:09:01.220 --> 01:09:07.740
He said, we shot that scene on a Friday night, and it had been 78 degrees that day.

1410
01:09:08.320 --> 01:09:16.060
He said, all of the snow effects for this episode, filling the parking lot as well as that little snowfall behind our windows,

1411
01:09:16.080 --> 01:09:20.400
that cost $36,000 for snow.

1412
01:09:20.600 --> 01:09:24.300
For snow on a day where it was almost 80 degrees.

1413
01:09:24.920 --> 01:09:32.720
You know, it's always fascinating to me when Randy shares with us how much something cost in an episode because it's like—

1414
01:09:32.720 --> 01:09:34.360
He had to budget all of that.

1415
01:09:34.520 --> 01:09:38.040
Yeah, and the budget for each episode does not change.

1416
01:09:38.040 --> 01:09:39.860
It doesn't change week to week, but the demands change.

1417
01:09:40.300 --> 01:09:45.320
So he would have this elaborate spreadsheet, and he would have to figure out how to make all these little details happen.

1418
01:09:45.540 --> 01:09:51.580
He needed to get the geese and the swans and the special prop and the snow and the drumline

1419
01:09:51.580 --> 01:09:55.660
and make it all cost the same as an episode that didn't even have all those things.

1420
01:09:55.840 --> 01:09:56.040
Right.

1421
01:09:56.260 --> 01:09:57.680
It's really fascinating to me.

1422
01:09:58.020 --> 01:09:59.420
But you know I love a spreadsheet.

1423
01:09:59.640 --> 01:10:00.460
I know you do.

1424
01:10:01.040 --> 01:10:11.180
Well, you know, Mindy was talking in the commentary about the scene about how hard Ed had to work as Andy doing the running man dancing with the cymbals.

1425
01:10:11.380 --> 01:10:14.980
And we did a lot of takes, and she was like, do you guys remember?

1426
01:10:14.980 --> 01:10:15.840
He was, like, sweating.

1427
01:10:17.700 --> 01:10:19.000
He did such a good job.

1428
01:10:19.000 --> 01:10:19.580
I know.

1429
01:10:20.100 --> 01:10:22.620
Well, that is how the episode ends.

1430
01:10:23.140 --> 01:10:27.900
However, we got a fan question from Katie G.

1431
01:10:28.260 --> 01:10:29.920
in Greenville, South Carolina.

1432
01:10:30.380 --> 01:10:39.480
In the DVD commentary, Mindy teases that Greg had an alternate ending for this episode, but she was not going to tell us what it was.

1433
01:10:40.220 --> 01:10:42.760
She kind of just says, you'll have to ask Greg.

1434
01:10:43.040 --> 01:10:43.360
Yeah.

1435
01:10:44.280 --> 01:10:46.980
What was the ending, Katie wants to know?

1436
01:10:47.220 --> 01:10:49.080
Well, Katie, I heard this conversation, too.

1437
01:10:49.320 --> 01:10:52.640
It happens as the credits, you know, are going at the end.

1438
01:10:52.740 --> 01:10:53.020
Mm-hmm.

1439
01:10:53.400 --> 01:10:58.160
And our editor, Claire Scanlon, jumps in and says she actually cut out the scene.

1440
01:10:58.400 --> 01:11:01.180
Well, listen, we wanted to get to the bottom of this.

1441
01:11:01.320 --> 01:11:06.040
We wanted to mom detectives this and actually solve this one.

1442
01:11:06.040 --> 01:11:07.200
Will we do it?

1443
01:11:07.200 --> 01:11:12.680
I started by reaching out to Greg Daniels, and he did not remember.

1444
01:11:13.040 --> 01:11:19.360
I reached out to Claire Scanlon, who enlisted the help of Rob Burnett, the office writer's assistant.

1445
01:11:19.860 --> 01:11:28.080
Rob went through his digital clutter and found a tag in a late rewrite of the script, and then Claire found the footage.

1446
01:11:28.640 --> 01:11:29.640
That's right.

1447
01:11:29.640 --> 01:11:36.900
It is a scene where Pam sees Oscar and Matt walking away from the drumline group.

1448
01:11:37.200 --> 01:11:38.540
They're going to go back inside.

1449
01:11:39.060 --> 01:11:47.160
Pam follows them in, but then quickly comes back out of the building, and it is clear that she has seen them making out.

1450
01:11:47.160 --> 01:11:53.620
So that was the surprise ending that Matt and Oscar hook up at work.

1451
01:11:53.620 --> 01:12:05.940
Now, this is very funny to me, Angela, because Pam has now caught Phyllis and Bob in a bathroom at lunch, and now she has caught Matt and Oscar in the hall closet.

1452
01:12:06.240 --> 01:12:10.720
I mean, how many people are getting it on at work or during work hours?

1453
01:12:10.720 --> 01:12:12.680
Well, we haven't even brought up Kelly and Ryan.

1454
01:12:12.980 --> 01:12:20.340
And then, of course, Dwight and Angela, don't get me started, but Pam and Jim had a pretty steamy kiss one time after hours.

1455
01:12:20.580 --> 01:12:21.300
Well, that's true.

1456
01:12:21.460 --> 01:12:28.140
Well, you know later, it's sort of speculated that Jim and Pam make their second baby at work.

1457
01:12:28.180 --> 01:12:28.760
Oh, God.

1458
01:12:28.800 --> 01:12:30.040
It's on Valentine's Day.

1459
01:12:30.220 --> 01:12:32.740
Right, and then Michael and Holly are in the stairwell.

1460
01:12:32.740 --> 01:12:34.500
Yeah, they forgot to lock the door.

1461
01:12:34.660 --> 01:12:36.980
That leads to the entire robbery of Crime Aid.

1462
01:12:37.200 --> 01:12:39.640
I mean, this place is a hotbed of hookups.

1463
01:12:40.300 --> 01:12:41.840
A hotbed of hookups.

1464
01:12:42.720 --> 01:12:43.560
Wait a second.

1465
01:12:44.080 --> 01:12:46.480
Did mom detectives just solve one?

1466
01:12:46.580 --> 01:12:49.700
We solved one, and I feel very good about solving one.

1467
01:12:50.380 --> 01:12:53.220
I am so excited that we got to the bottom of that.

1468
01:12:53.580 --> 01:12:55.920
Claire and Rob, thank you so much.

1469
01:12:56.160 --> 01:12:56.480
Yes.

1470
01:12:56.640 --> 01:12:58.240
They sent us this footage, you guys.

1471
01:12:58.340 --> 01:12:59.120
It's pretty special.

1472
01:12:59.440 --> 01:13:02.320
The cast ends up in a snowball fight once again.

1473
01:13:02.320 --> 01:13:02.900
Yeah.

1474
01:13:03.140 --> 01:13:04.240
It really brought me back.

1475
01:13:04.520 --> 01:13:06.640
